X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:03.303
In this lesson, you will

2
00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:07.540
have a brief recap of HNSW,
the foundation of vector databases,

3
00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:10.977
which enables efficient nearest-neighbor
searches.

4
00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:14.414
You will learn how to manage
and then enhance

5
00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:16.983
the quality of the results it delivers.

6
00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:18.752
Let's go!

7
00:00:18.752 --> 00:00:21.354
Most of the vector databases use HNSW

8
00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:24.357
to approximate
the nearest-neighbor search.

9
00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:29.195
There are certain ways in which we can
control how well this approximation works.

10
00:00:29.696 --> 00:00:32.432
The hierarchical navigable Small Words

11
00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:36.269
is a multi-layer graph
that approximates nearest-neighbor search.

12
00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:39.706
It uses vectors as nodes connected

13
00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:42.709
to each other based on their similarity.

14
00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:46.713
For that reason, the similarity measure
has to be selected upfront.

15
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.983
This data structure builds several stacked

16
00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:53.186
graphs, with the top
one being the most sparse.

17
00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.123
This one contains
just a small subset of all the vectors.

18
00:00:57.424 --> 00:01:01.628
Each consecutive layer contains
more and more, but the rule is

19
00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:06.366
all the vectors from the layer above
are also present in the current layer.

20
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:11.004
The bottom layer is really dense
as it contains all the vectors we have.

21
00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:14.707
Except for the similarity-based edges,

22
00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:17.710
there are also internal layer connections

23
00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:21.014
that help us jump between the layers.

24
00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:25.518
There are created between the same vectors
just at the different layers.

25
00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:29.689
HNSW has two parameters
that we can control.

26
00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:34.094
The first one, M, defines
how many edges per node we create.

27
00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:39.365
Increasing this parameter's value
should improve the approximation quality,

28
00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:42.635
but it won't fix any problems
with your embedding model.

29
00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:46.606
If your representations are just too weak,
you cannot expect

30
00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:50.043
search to derive the semantics
of the data magically.

31
00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:54.180
Higher values of M
will just bring you closer to the exact

32
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.682
k nearest-neighbor search.

33
00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:59.786
However, controlling this parameter
may also help to adjust latency.

34
00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:03.056
There are some cases
where you can sacrifice the quality

35
00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:05.191
for faster retrieval.

36
00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:10.063
The most common operation performed with
help of the HNSW graph is search.

37
00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:14.434
When we receive a query,
it is also vectorized using the same

38
00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:18.371
embedding model and create the vector acts
as a real query.

39
00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:21.741
The search operation
starts from the top layer.

40
00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:26.012
Here the algorithm finds the ef closest
points.

41
00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:30.183
Ef is the second parameter
we can consider. In our case,

42
00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:33.253
its value was set to two, so

43
00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:36.389
this is how many points
we select in the first layer.

44
00:02:37.123 --> 00:02:40.593
If we selected more,
we could possibly get better results,

45
00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.863
but it would also make the process slower.

46
00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:47.467
The points selected in the top layer
are then selected

47
00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:50.970
in the layer below,
thanks to the intra layer edges.

48
00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:54.874
We do not traverse all the points here,
but only focus

49
00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:58.711
on the direct neighbors of the points
selected in this previous step.

50
00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:02.081
Again, we select ef closest

51
00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:05.084
matches, then the process continues.

52
00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:09.222
Repeat the same procedure
until we reach the bottom layer.

53
00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:14.856
Each time we only check the previously
selected points and their direct

54
00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:16.296
neighbors.

55
00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:19.299
That effectively narrows
down the scope of search.

56
00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:23.102
In the last layer,
we select the top k entries

57
00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:27.640
and these vectors become the most
relevant documents that we return.

58
00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.743
K might be different from ef
and it's usually lower.

59
00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:35.148
Let's see how changing both parameters
might impact the search quality

60
00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:36.816
on the once dataset

61
00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:40.887
you already know. You will use the
collection built in the previous lesson

62
00:03:40.887 --> 00:03:44.857
and try to improve the quality
by controlling the HSNW parameters.

63
00:03:45.158 --> 00:03:49.229
The only difference is that this
collection will be loaded from snapshot,

64
00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:54.529
and it will contain the vectors of all
the products available in one dataset.

65
00:03:55.535 --> 00:03:58.538
So you don't have to recompute the metrics
again.

66
00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:02.775
The process of importing the snapshots
may also take a while.

67
00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:07.046
If you haven't modified the collection
configuration,

68
00:04:07.113 --> 00:04:10.450
then all the HSNW parameters
should be set to the defaults.

69
00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:14.787
It might be accessed in the HSNW config
property.

70
00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:20.255
In our case, M is set to 16 and EF to 100.

71
00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:23.463
Let's start with the evaluation.

72
00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:26.366
You will only recompute the embeddings
of all the queries,

73
00:04:26.366 --> 00:04:29.802
as we still need them to test
various configurations of the collection.

74
00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.772
Let's load the model.

75
00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:34.874
Once the model is ready,

76
00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:37.877
we can also load the set of queries

77
00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:41.347
and create embeddings
for every single one.

78
00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.953
Vector databases
approximate nearest-neighbor search

79
00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:50.056
and that comes with some quality
degradation.

80
00:04:51.224 --> 00:04:53.293
The default search operation runs

81
00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:56.296
the approximated search by default.

82
00:04:57.563 --> 00:05:00.566
We can also enable the exact mode

83
00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:05.171
to force the system
to run pure K, and then search.

84
00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:07.740
That won't be as

85
00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:11.377
fast, but you will always
get the highest quality possible

86
00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:14.847
given the selected embedding model.

87
00:05:17.083 --> 00:05:19.385
HSNW configuration changes wont

88
00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:21.587
improve
the quality of the embedding model.

89
00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:26.025
It can only control how
well the approximation does its job.

90
00:05:26.359 --> 00:05:29.429
The ground
truth dataset will no longer be built

91
00:05:29.429 --> 00:05:32.532
based on the perfect matches
for the benchmark queries,

92
00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:36.736
but you will calculate the quality
of the approximation itself.

93
00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:40.573
For that reason,
let's build a Qrels object

94
00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.943
with the exact search mode enabled.

95
00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:47.013
This will be our reference
to measure the effectiveness

96
00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:49.816
of various HSNW configurations.

97
00:05:49.816 --> 00:05:54.816
Everything starts with iterating over
the set of queries we have. For each query,

98
00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:59.492
we run the search operation
in the exact mode

99
00:05:59.826 --> 00:06:04.826
so it's pure knn, and then eventually
we iterate over the set of results

100
00:06:06.265 --> 00:06:08.234
returned by the system and

101
00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:11.971
build the corresponding dictionary

102
00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:15.408
that is
then used to create the Qrels object.

103
00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:28.388
Our Qrels are ready
and the baseline will be defined by how

104
00:06:28.388 --> 00:06:33.059
well the approximation works
with the current default configuration.

105
00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:37.663
This time we are performing
the same operations like for the knn

106
00:06:37.663 --> 00:06:41.634
and then search,
but the exact mode is set to false.

107
00:06:51.644 --> 00:06:54.847
You will now evaluate the first run
using ranx again.

108
00:06:55.181 --> 00:06:59.619
It doesn't make much sense to calculate
metric different from precision at k,

109
00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:03.222
as the scores and documents
ranx want to change.

110
00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:07.026
Embedding
model still produces the same embeddings,

111
00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:11.164
but some of the results might be just
missing compared to exact knn.

112
00:07:12.565 --> 00:07:14.734
The current default configuration

113
00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:18.204
of our collection
achieves the precision at 25.

114
00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:22.241
At around 0.998.

115
00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:24.577
Even though the

116
00:07:24.577 --> 00:07:28.214
precision at 25 seems to be really high,

117
00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:31.751
increasing the values of both parameters

118
00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:34.887
should result in quality improvement.

119
00:07:34.887 --> 00:07:38.891
You can experiment with various numbers
or even decrease them

120
00:07:39.025 --> 00:07:42.028
if your main concern is speed of search.

121
00:07:44.263 --> 00:07:46.599
Let's wait for Qdrant

122
00:07:46.599 --> 00:07:49.602
to finish building
the new HSNW graph.

123
00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:58.110
We need to wait until Qdrant

124
00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:01.113
finishes the optimization process
and build

125
00:08:01.681 --> 00:08:04.684
the newly updated HSNW structures.

126
00:08:04.917 --> 00:08:07.887
Otherwise,
we might be still using the old ones

127
00:08:07.887 --> 00:08:11.557
and get the same quality measures.
As a next step,

128
00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:14.861
you will perform the same search process
like before.

129
00:08:15.495 --> 00:08:18.531
Nothing changes regarding
how you call Qdrant.

130
00:08:19.632 --> 00:08:21.901
But the newly updated parameters

131
00:08:21.901 --> 00:08:24.904
should impact the results of each query.

132
00:08:25.171 --> 00:08:29.008
This cell should result
in another run object being created.

133
00:08:38.284 --> 00:08:39.919
As a final step,

134
00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:42.154
you will calculate the same metric

135
00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:45.157
but using new parameters.

136
00:08:45.391 --> 00:08:48.761
It shouldn't be surprising that
the quality of our results has improved.

137
00:08:49.095 --> 00:08:52.098
Maybe we didn't have to increase
the values that much.

138
00:08:52.365 --> 00:08:56.202
In the real-world applications,
keeping them at a minimum sensible

139
00:08:56.202 --> 00:09:01.007
level makes a lot of sense, as high values
also come with a cost.

140
00:09:01.908 --> 00:09:05.745
The higher there are, the slower
the search operations will be.

141
00:09:06.612 --> 00:09:09.181
Also, the number of edges in a graph

142
00:09:09.181 --> 00:09:12.585
increases its size,
so that's a memory overhead.

143
00:09:13.019 --> 00:09:16.055
One important detail
about how vector databases

144
00:09:16.055 --> 00:09:20.126
implementation HSNW is
that it's usually divided into segments.

145
00:09:20.259 --> 00:09:23.162
So there is no single global graph.

146
00:09:23.162 --> 00:09:26.966
In most vector databases,
the HSNW graph is not created globally,

147
00:09:26.966 --> 00:09:30.369
but points are divided
into non-overlapping segments.

148
00:09:31.037 --> 00:09:34.473
Each segments
will have a separate structure created.

149
00:09:34.707 --> 00:09:38.144
It helps a lot from the scalability
perspective, as these segments

150
00:09:38.144 --> 00:09:41.213
may be distributed
across a whole class of machines.

151
00:09:41.247 --> 00:09:45.518
That approach also improves the ability
of the system to work concurrently

152
00:09:45.518 --> 00:09:50.189
in a single-node scenario,
using multiple CPU cores simultaneously.

153
00:09:50.723 --> 00:09:55.394
It's also way easier
to rebuild this single segment

154
00:09:55.394 --> 00:09:59.398
HSNW graphs,
because we can do it one by one.

155
00:09:59.899 --> 00:10:03.836
In this lesson, you learned that
controlling the parameters

156
00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:07.573
of HSNW graph may also give you

157
00:10:07.573 --> 00:10:11.777
an additional boost
in terms of the search quality.

158
00:10:12.244 --> 00:10:16.282
You can play with different values
of both parameters available

159
00:10:16.582 --> 00:10:20.019
to see how they will impact it even more.

160
00:10:20.186 --> 00:10:23.189
In the next lesson, you will learn
some additional optimization

161
00:10:23.189 --> 00:10:26.192
techniques that can help you reduce
memory requirements

162
00:10:26.525 --> 00:10:29.528
and sometimes even boost retrieval speed.

163
00:10:30.096 --> 00:10:30.663
See you there!

